Find the standard equation of the ellipse that satisfies the following conditions:
(1) The foci are on the x-axis and the ellipse passes through points (0,1) and (3,0);
(2) The eccentricity is $\frac{3}{5}$, and the length of the minor axis is 8.

Let the complex number $Z=\lg (m^{2}-2m-2)+(m^{2}+3m+2)i$. Find the values of $m$ for which:  
$(1) Z$ is a real number;  
$(2) Z$ is a pure imaginary number;  
$(3) The point corresponding to $Z$ is located in the first quadrant of the complex plane.

Given an ellipse $C$: $\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \frac{y^2}{4} = 1$ ($a > 2$), a point $P$ on it has a total distance of $6$ to its two foci $F_1$ (left) and $F_2$ (right).  
$(1)$ Find the eccentricity of the ellipse $C$;  
$(2)$ If $PF_2$ is perpendicular to the x-axis, and the projection of $P$ on the y-axis is point $Q$, find the coordinates of point $Q$.

Given the following four pairs of vectors:  
① $\vec{a}=(1,-2,1)$, $\vec{b}=(-1,2,-1)$;  
② $\vec{a}=(8,4,0)$, $\vec{b}=(2,1,0)$;  
③ $\vec{a}=(1,0,-1)$, $\vec{b}=(-3,0,3)$;  
④ $\vec{a}=(-\frac{4}{3},1,-1)$, $\vec{b}=(4,-3,3)$  
Which pairs are parallel to each other?  
A: ②③  
B: ①④  
C: ①②④  
D: ①②③④

Given an ellipse $C$: $\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}}+ \frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}}=1 (a > b > 0)$ with an eccentricity of $e= \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$, its left and right foci are $F_{1}$ and $F_{2}$, respectively, with $|F_{1}F_{2}|=2\sqrt{3}$. Let points $M(x_{1},y_{1})$ and $N(x_{2},y_{2})$ be two distinct points on the ellipse, and the product of the slopes of the lines connecting these points to the origin is $- \frac{1}{4}$.
(I) Find the equation of the ellipse $C$;
(II) Prove that $x_{1}^{2}+x_{2}^{2}$ is a constant value and find that value.

Given vectors $\overrightarrow{a}=(1,-2)$ and $\overrightarrow{b}=(3,4)$:  
$(1)$ If $(3 \overrightarrow{a}- \overrightarrow{b})\parallel(\overrightarrow{a}+k \overrightarrow{b})$, find the value of the real number $k$;  
$(2)$ If $\overrightarrow{a}\perp(m \overrightarrow{a}- \overrightarrow{b})$, find the value of the real number $m$.

The number of incorrect conclusions is\\((\\)　　\\()\\)  
\\(①\\) "If \\(a=0\\)," is a necessary but not sufficient condition for the complex number \\(a+bi(a,b∈R)\\) to be a pure imaginary number;  
\\(②\\) Proposition \\(p\\): For all \\(x∈[0,1]\\), \\(e^{x}≥1\\), Proposition \\(q\\): There exists an \\(x∈R\\) such that \\(x^{2}+x+1 < 0\\), then \\(p∨q\\) is true;  
\\(③\\) The converse of "If \\(am^{2} < bm^{2}\\), then \\(a < b\\)" is a true statement;  
\\(④\\) If \\(p∨q\\) is a false statement, then both \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are false statements.  
A: \\(0\\)  
B: \\(1\\)  
C: \\(2\\)  
D: \\(3\\)

Consider the hyperbola $C_{(a,b)}$: $\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}}-\frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}}=1 (a,b > 0)$. If a point $P(x_{0},y_{0})$ satisfies $\frac{x_{0}^{2}}{a^{2}}-\frac{y_{0}^{2}}{b^{2}} < 1$, it is said to be in the exterior of $C_{(a,b)}$; if a point $P(x_{0},y_{0})$ satisfies $\frac{x_{0}^{2}}{a^{2}}-\frac{y_{0}^{2}}{b^{2}} > 1$, it is said to be in the interior of $C_{(a,b)}$:

1. Prove that all points on the line $3x-y+1=0$ are in the exterior of $C_{(1,1)}$;
2. If the coordinates of point $M$ are $(0,-1)$ and point $N$ is in the interior or on $C_{(1,1)}$, find the minimum value of $|\overrightarrow{MN}|$;
3. If $C_{(a,b)}$ passes through the point $(2,1)$, and the length of the circular arc formed by the points inside $C_{(a,b)}$ and on the circle $x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2} (r > 0)$ is equal to half the circumference of the circle, find the relationship between $b$ and $r$, and the range of $r$.

Suppose a 2nd-order matrix $M$ satisfies $M \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4\\end{pmatrix}= \begin{pmatrix} 7 & 10 \\ 4 & 6\\end{pmatrix}$.

1. Find the 2nd-order matrix $M$;
2. Apply the transformation corresponding to matrix $M$ to the curve $3x^{2}+8xy+6y^{2}=1$, and find the equation of the resulting curve.

Given the parabola C<sub>1</sub>: $y^{2} = 2px$ and a point M(3, $y_0$) on it, the distance from M to the focus F of the parabola is 4. Also, there is an ellipse C<sub>2</sub>: $\frac{y^{2}}{a^{2}} + \frac{x^{2}}{b^{2}} = 1$ $(a > b > 0)$ with eccentricity $e = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$, and the ellipse passes through the focus F of the parabola.

(1) Find the standard equations of the parabola C<sub>1</sub> and the ellipse C<sub>2</sub>;
(2) A line l<sub>1</sub> passes through the focus F and intersects the parabola C<sub>1</sub> at two different points A and B, and intersects the y-axis at point N. Given $\vec{NA} = \lambda \vec{AF}$ and $\vec{NB} = \mu \vec{BF}$, prove that $\lambda + \mu$ is a constant.

For what real number values of $m$ does the complex number $z=(m+2)+(3-2m)i$ satisfy the following conditions:

(1) It is the conjugate of the complex number $12+17i$;

(2) The modulus of the complex number is at its minimum, and find this minimum value.

Given vectors $a=(-3,2)$, $b=(2,1)$, and $c=(3,-1)$.

(1) Find the minimum value of $|a+tb|$ and the corresponding value of $t$;

(2) If $a-tb$ is collinear with $c$, find the value of the real number $t$.

Given an ellipse $E$: $\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1 (a > b > 0)$ with a vertex at $A(0,1)$ and a focal length of $2\sqrt{3}$.  
$(1)$ Find the equation of the ellipse $E$;  
$(2)$ A line passing through point $P(0,-2)$ with a slope of $k$ intersects the ellipse $E$ at two distinct points $B$ and $C$, and $|BC| = \frac{4\sqrt{2}}{5}$. Find the value of $k$.

Given that $F_{1}(-c,0)$ and $F_{2}(c, 0)$ are the left and right foci of the ellipse $G$: $\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}}+\frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}}=1 (0 < b < a < 3)$, and point $P(2, \sqrt{2})$ is a point on ellipse $G$ with $|PF_{1}|-|PF_{2}|=a$.

(1) Find the equation of ellipse $G$;

(2) If line $l$ intersects ellipse $G$ at points $A$ and $B$, and $\overrightarrow{OA} \perp \overrightarrow{OB}$, where $O$ is the coordinate origin, determine whether the distance from $O$ to line $l$ is a constant value. If it is, find that value; if not, explain why.

Here are four propositions about necessary and sufficient conditions:
1. The necessary and sufficient condition for vector $\mathbf{b}$ to be collinear with a nonzero vector $\mathbf{a}$ is that there exists exactly one real number $\lambda$ such that $\mathbf{b} = \lambda\mathbf{a}$;
2. The necessary and sufficient condition for $a$, $b$, $c$ to form a geometric sequence is $b^2 = ac$;
3. Two events being mutually exclusive is the necessary and sufficient condition for these two events to be complementary;
4. The necessary and sufficient condition for the function $y = x^2 + bx + c$ to be an even function is $b = 0$.

Among these, the numbers of the true propositions are: (Write down the numbers of all true propositions)

Given an ellipse \\(C\\) with its center at the origin \\(O\\), its foci on the \\(x\\)-axis, and its eccentricity as \\(\dfrac{1}{2}\\), the distance from the right focus to the right vertex is \\(1\\).  
\\((1)\\) Find the standard equation of the ellipse \\(C\\);  
\\((2)\\) Does there exist a line \\(l\\) intersecting ellipse \\(C\\) at points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) with the equation \\(y=kx+m(k∈R)\\), such that \\(|\overrightarrow{OA}+2\overrightarrow{OB}|=|\overrightarrow{OA}-2\overrightarrow{OB}|\) holds? If it exists, find the range of the real number \\(m\\); if not, explain why.

Given that one of the foci of a hyperbola is $(4,0)$, and its eccentricity $e=2$.  
$(1)$ Find the standard equation of the hyperbola;  
$(2)$ Write the equation of the asymptotes of the hyperbola, and calculate the distance from its focus $(4,0)$ to the other asymptote.

Given that the right focus of the ellipse $C$: $\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}}+ \frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}}=1(a > b > 0)$ is $F(1,0)$, and the point $A(2,0)$ is on the ellipse. The line $l$ passing through $F(1,0)$ intersects the ellipse $C$ at two distinct points $M$ and $N$.
(1) Find the equation of the ellipse $C$;
(2) If the slope of line $l$ is $1$, find the length of the line segment $MN$;
(3) If the perpendicular bisector of line segment $MN$ intersects the $y$-axis at point $P(0,y_{0})$, find the range of $y_{0}$.

Given an ellipse $C$ with its center at the origin and its foci on the $x$-axis, the focal distance is $4\sqrt{3}$, and the eccentricity is $\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$  
$(1)$ Find the equation of the ellipse $C$;  
$(2)$ Suppose a line $L$ passes through the point $M(0,1)$ and intersects the ellipse $C$ at points $A$ and $B$. If $\overrightarrow{AM}=2\overrightarrow{MB}$, find the equation of line $L$.

Given the complex number $z=3+bi$ ($b\in R$), and $(1+3i)\cdot z$ is a purely imaginary number.

(1) Find the complex number $z$;

(2) If $\omega = \frac{z}{2+i}$, find the modulus of the complex number $\omega$ ($|\omega |$).

Given that $z$ is a complex number, and $z-3i$ is a real number, $\frac{z-5i}{2-i}$ is a pure imaginary number (where $i$ is the imaginary unit).  
(I) Find the complex number $z$;  
(II) Find the modulus of $\frac{z}{1-i}$.

In the plane, the product of the slopes of the lines connecting point P with two fixed points $A_1(-a, 0)$ and $A_2(a, 0)$ (where $a > 0$) is a non-zero constant $m$. It is known that the eccentricity of the trajectory $C$ of point $P$ is $\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$.  
(1) Find the value of $m$;  
(2) Find the intersection points $A$ and $B$ of the ellipse $C$ with a line passing through the right focus of the ellipse $C$ and having a slope of $1$. If $O$ is the origin and $M$ is a point on the ellipse $C$ satisfying $\overrightarrow{OM} = \lambda \overrightarrow{OA} + \overrightarrow{OB}$, find the value of $\lambda$.

Let $$\overrightarrow{a}$$ be a known planar vector and $$\overrightarrow{a} \neq \overrightarrow{0}$$, regarding the decomposition of vector $$\overrightarrow{a}$$, the following four propositions are given:  
① Given vector $$\overrightarrow{b}$$, there always exists a vector $$\overrightarrow{c}$$ such that $$\overrightarrow{a} = \overrightarrow{b} + \overrightarrow{c}$$;  
② Given vectors $$\overrightarrow{b}$$ and $$\overrightarrow{c}$$, there always exist real numbers λ and μ such that $$\overrightarrow{a} = λ\overrightarrow{b} + μ\overrightarrow{c}$$;  
③ Given a unit vector $$\overrightarrow{b}$$ and a positive number μ, there always exists a unit vector $$\overrightarrow{c}$$ and a real number λ such that $$\overrightarrow{a} = λ\overrightarrow{b} + μ\overrightarrow{c}$$;  
④ Given positive numbers λ and μ, there always exist unit vectors $$\overrightarrow{b}$$ and $$\overrightarrow{c}$$ such that $$\overrightarrow{a} = λ\overrightarrow{b} + μ\overrightarrow{c}$$;  
The vectors $$\overrightarrow{b}$$, $$\overrightarrow{c}$$, and $$\overrightarrow{a}$$ mentioned in the above propositions are in the same plane and are not collinear with each other. The number of true propositions is (　　)  
A: 1  
B: 2  
C: 3  
D: 4

Given an ellipse $\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}} + \frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}} = 1$ $(a > b > 0)$ with a focal length of $2\sqrt{2}$, and a line $l$ passing through the point $(-\sqrt{2}, 1)$ with a slope of $k$, intersecting the ellipse at points $A$ and $B$, and the $x$-axis at point $P$. Let $C$ be the reflection of point $A$ over the $x$-axis, and $Q$ be the intersection of line $BC$ and the $x$-axis.

(1) Find the range of values for $k$;

(2) Determine whether $|OP| \cdot |OQ|$ is a constant value. If it is, find the value; otherwise, provide an explanation.

Given an ellipse $C$ with its center at the origin and its foci on the $x$-axis, the focal distance is $4$, and the eccentricity is $\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$.
$(1)$ Find the equation of the ellipse $C$;
$(2)$ Given a line $l$ passing through point $P(0,-1)$ and intersecting the ellipse at points $A$ and $B$. If $\overrightarrow{AP}=2\overrightarrow{PB}$, find the equation of line $l$.

Given the complex number $z=(3+bi)(1+3i)$ ($b\in \mathbb{R}$) is a pure imaginary number.

$(1)$ Find the value of $b$;

$(2)$ If $\omega= \frac{3+bi}{2+i}$, find the modulus of the complex number $\omega$.

Let $\overset{ .}{z}$ be the conjugate of the complex number $z$, satisfying $|z- \overset{ .}{z}|=2 \sqrt {3}$.  
(1) If $z$ is a pure imaginary number, find $z$;  
(2) If $z- \overset{ .}{z}^2$ is a real number, find $|z|$.

By analogy from the multiplication laws of algebraic expressions, derive the operation laws for the scalar product (dot product) of vectors:  
① From "mn=nm", we deduce " $\overrightarrow{a} \cdot \overrightarrow{b} = \overrightarrow{b} \cdot \overrightarrow{a}$";  
② From "(m+n)t=mt+nt", we deduce " $(\overrightarrow{a} + \overrightarrow{b}) \cdot \overrightarrow{c} = \overrightarrow{a} \cdot \overrightarrow{c} + \overrightarrow{b} \cdot \overrightarrow{c}$";  
③ From "t≠0, mt=nt ⇒ m=n", we deduce " $\overrightarrow{c} ≠ \overrightarrow{0}, \overrightarrow{a} \cdot \overrightarrow{c} = \overrightarrow{b} \cdot \overrightarrow{c} ⇒ \overrightarrow{a} = \overrightarrow{b}$";  
④ From "|m \cdot n| = |m| \cdot |n|", we deduce "$|\overrightarrow{a} \cdot \overrightarrow{b}| = |\overrightarrow{a}| \cdot |\overrightarrow{b}|$";  
⑤ From "(m \cdot n)t = m(n \cdot t)", we deduce " $(\overrightarrow{a} \cdot \overrightarrow{b}) \cdot \overrightarrow{c} = \overrightarrow{a} (\overrightarrow{b} \cdot \overrightarrow{c})$";  
⑥ From "$\frac{ac}{bc} = \frac{a}{b}$", we deduce "$\frac{\overrightarrow{a} \cdot \overrightarrow{c}}{\overrightarrow{b} \cdot \overrightarrow{c}} = \frac{\overrightarrow{a}}{\overrightarrow{b}}$".  
Among the analogies made above, the correct conclusions are ______.

Among the following four propositions:
① The contrapositive of "If $xy=0$, then $x=0$ and $y=0$";
② "If $m>2$, then the solution set of the inequality $x^2-2x+m>0$ is $\mathbb{R}$";
③ If $F_1$ and $F_2$ are fixed points, $|F_1F_2|=7$, and a moving point $M$ satisfies $|MF_1|+|MF_2|=7$, then the trajectory of $M$ is an ellipse;
④ If $\{\vec{a}, \vec{b}, \vec{c}\}$ is a set of bases in space, then $\{\vec{a}+\vec{b}, \vec{b}+\vec{c}, \vec{c}+\vec{a}\}$ forms another set of bases in space;
The number of true propositions is (　　)
A: 1
B: 2
C: 3
D: 4

Given the ellipse $C:\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1 (a > b > 0)$ has an eccentricity of $\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$, and the point $(2,\sqrt{2})$ is on the ellipse.

$(1)$ Find the equation of the ellipse $C$;

$(2)$ Let $N(0,2)$, and a line $l$ passes through the point $P(-1,-2)$ and intersects the ellipse $C$ at two points $A$ and $B$ different from $N$, with the slopes of lines $NA$ and $NB$ being $k_1$ and $k_2$ respectively. The question is: Is $k_1+k_2$ a constant? If so, find the constant; if not, please explain why.

Given proposition p: The graph of $y=x+m-2$ does not pass through the second quadrant, and proposition q: The equation $x^2+ \frac{y^2}{1-m}=1$ represents an ellipse with foci on the x-axis.
(Ⅰ) Determine what condition p is for q;
(Ⅱ) If $p \land q$ is a false proposition and $p \lor q$ is a true proposition, find the range of the real number $m$.

The blue parabola shown is the graph of the equation $x = ay^2 + by + c$. Find $c$. The grid lines are spaced one unit apart.

[asy]
size(150);
real ticklen=3;
real tickspace=2;

real ticklength=0.1cm;
real axisarrowsize=0.14cm;
pen axispen=black+1.3bp;
real vectorarrowsize=0.2cm;
real tickdown=-0.5;
real tickdownlength=-0.15inch;
real tickdownbase=0.3;
real wholetickdown=tickdown;
void rr_cartesian_axes(real xleft, real xright, real ybottom, real ytop, real xstep=1, real ystep=1, bool

useticks=false, bool complexplane=false, bool usegrid=true) {

import graph;

real i;

if(complexplane) {

label("$\textnormal{Re}$",(xright,0),SE);

label("$\textnormal{Im}$",(0,ytop),NW);

} else {

label("$x$",(xright+0.4,-0.5));

label("$y$",(-0.5,ytop+0.2));

}

ylimits(ybottom,ytop);

xlimits( xleft, xright);

real[] TicksArrx,TicksArry;

for(i=xleft+xstep; i<xright; i+=xstep) {

if(abs(i) >0.1) {

TicksArrx.push(i);

}

}

for(i=ybottom+ystep; i<ytop; i+=ystep) {

if(abs(i) >0.1) {

TicksArry.push(i);

}

}

if(usegrid) {

xaxis(BottomTop(extend=false), Ticks("%", TicksArrx ,pTick=gray

(0.22),extend=true),p=invisible);//,above=true);

yaxis(LeftRight(extend=false),Ticks("%", TicksArry ,pTick=gray(0.22),extend=true),

p=invisible);//,Arrows);

}

if(useticks) {

xequals(0, ymin=ybottom, ymax=ytop, p=axispen, Ticks("%",TicksArry ,

pTick=black+0.8bp,Size=ticklength), above=true, Arrows(size=axisarrowsize));

yequals(0, xmin=xleft, xmax=xright, p=axispen, Ticks("%",TicksArrx ,

pTick=black+0.8bp,Size=ticklength), above=true, Arrows(size=axisarrowsize));

} else {

xequals(0, ymin=ybottom, ymax=ytop, p=axispen, above=true, Arrows(size=axisarrowsize));

yequals(0, xmin=xleft, xmax=xright, p=axispen, above=true, Arrows(size=axisarrowsize));

}
};
real lowerx, upperx, lowery, uppery;
real f(real x) {return (x-1)*(x-1)/3-3;}
lowery = -1;
uppery = 5;
rr_cartesian_axes(-6,3,lowery,uppery);
draw(reflect((0,0),(1,1))*(graph(f,lowery,uppery,operator ..)), blue);
[/asy]

